WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:09.760 align:start position:19%
What if I told you that right now, above
your head, the universe is screaming?

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:15.480 align:start position:19%
Not with sound, with flashes of radio
light so violent they can briefly

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:21.360 align:start position:19%
outshine an entire galaxy.
Each one lasts less than the blink of an

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:27.240 align:start position:19%
eye, then silence.
Astronomers call them fast radio bursts.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:33.040 align:start position:19%
And almost 20 years after the first one
was found, they are still one of the

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:37.920 align:start position:19%
strangest mysteries in the sky.
Just think about this for a second.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:42.640 align:start position:19%
In a few thousandths of a second,
less time than a camera flash,

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:48.000 align:start position:19%
a single burst releases as much energy
as our sun produces in days.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:53.000 align:start position:19%
Some estimates say decades.
Its brightness reaches 10 to the 36

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:58.680 align:start position:19%
Kelvin. A number so extreme that no
ordinary matter should be able to glow

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:04.360 align:start position:19%
that hot, that fast. It blows my mind
that something smaller than a city can

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:09.120 align:start position:19%
briefly outshine 500 million suns.
And here's the wildest part. The first

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:14.360 align:start position:19%
one sat on a hard drive for 6 years
before anyone noticed.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:17.760 align:start position:19%
In 2007, astronomer Duncan Lorimer was
digging through old data from

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:23.040 align:start position:19%
Australia's Parkes telescope when he
found a single spike,

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:27.360 align:start position:19%
5 milliseconds long from 2001,
that did not match anything known.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:31.200 align:start position:19%
One flash,
billions of light years away,

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:36.280 align:start position:19%
then nothing.
The universe had screamed, and nobody

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:40.280 align:start position:19%
heard it until years later.
For years, every burst we found was a

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:45.880 align:start position:19%
one-time event.
A flash gone forever, never to repeat,

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:50.200 align:start position:19%
which raised an impossible question.
Whatever makes these things, does it

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:55.720 align:start position:19%
destroy itself in the process?
Are we watching cosmic explosions? The

00:01:52.960 --> 00:02:01.960 align:start position:19%
final scream of something dying?
And then, in 2012, one of them screamed

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:10.440 align:start position:19%
twice.
The repeater is called FRB 121102,

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:16.120 align:start position:19%
and it changed everything. It flashed,
then flashed again, then hundreds more

00:02:12.959 --> 00:02:21.800 align:start position:19%
times from a dwarf galaxy 3 billion
light-years away, right next to a

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:27.000 align:start position:19%
strange, persistent glow of radio light.
Nothing about it behaved like a dying

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:32.160 align:start position:19%
star. Whatever this thing was, it
survived its own screams, and it was

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:37.120 align:start position:19%
just getting warmed up.
Canada's CHIME telescope turned out to

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:41.800 align:start position:19%
be a burst-catching machine. Scientists
now estimate that somewhere between

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:48.000 align:start position:19%
1,000 and 10,000 fast radio bursts rip
across the entire sky every single day.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:52.160 align:start position:19%
We catch only a tiny fraction. The rest
scream into the dark, unheard,

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:57.840 align:start position:19%
unrecorded. The loudest show in the
universe playing to an empty theater.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:04.160 align:start position:19%
So, what could possibly be that
powerful?

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:09.880 align:start position:19%
Meet the prime suspect, the magnetar.
Picture the collapsed core of a massive

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:13.760 align:start position:19%
dead star, squeezed into a ball just 20
km wide,

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:19.120 align:start position:19%
the size of a city,
spinning hundreds of times per second,

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:25.040 align:start position:19%
wrapped in a magnetic field about 1
quadrillion times stronger than Earth's.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:28.320 align:start position:19%
A magnetar sitting halfway to the moon
would wipe every credit card on the

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:32.840 align:start position:19%
planet.
It is the most magnetic object known to

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:38.519 align:start position:19%
exist.
A magnetar does not sit quietly.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:41.480 align:start position:19%
Its solid crust cracks under magnetic
stress.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:45.640 align:start position:19%
Starquakes,
a trillion times more violent than any

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:51.600 align:start position:19%
earthquake.
Its magnetic field lines twist,

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:55.200 align:start position:19%
snap, and reconnect in milliseconds,
whipping particles to nearly the speed

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:59.080 align:start position:19%
of light.
The energy released in one of these

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:03.800 align:start position:19%
magnetic tantrums is almost beyond
comprehension.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:10.320 align:start position:19%
On paper, it looked like a perfect match
for a fast radio burst.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:10.320 align:start position:19%
But on paper is not proof.

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:22.000 align:start position:19%
Then came April 2020
and everything changed. A magnetar

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:27.040 align:start position:19%
called SGR 1935 + 2154
sitting inside our own Milky Way, just

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:31.800 align:start position:19%
30,000 light years away,
fired off a millisecond blast of radio

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:36.520 align:start position:19%
waves bright enough to look exactly like
a fast radio burst.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:40.960 align:start position:19%
For the first time in history,
we caught one red-handed.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:45.720 align:start position:19%
The prime suspect was standing over the
smoking gun

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:51.400 align:start position:19%
in our own galactic backyard.
That single flash rewrote the textbooks.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:54.840 align:start position:19%
At least some fast radio bursts, maybe
most,

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:59.240 align:start position:19%
come from magnetars having violent
magnetic outbursts.

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:04.640 align:start position:19%
The case seemed closed.
And honestly, it is a beautiful answer.

00:05:01.919 --> 00:05:09.520 align:start position:19%
Not aliens, not magic, just the most
extreme magnets in nature screaming

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:14.320 align:start position:19%
across the cosmos.
But the universe is always had one more

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:17.840 align:start position:19%
twist waiting.
The twist arrived from an impossible

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:22.720 align:start position:19%
address.
FRB 200120E

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:28.160 align:start position:19%
was traced to a globular cluster in the
galaxy M81.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:32.120 align:start position:19%
A swarm of ancient stars billions of
years old.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:36.360 align:start position:19%
Magnetars are born from young massive
stars that die fast.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:41.240 align:start position:19%
They should not exist in a retirement
home of old stars.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:46.240 align:start position:19%
Yet there it was screaming away.
Somehow a burst engine was running in

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:51.160 align:start position:19%
the last place anyone expected.
The mystery was not solved. It had just

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:56.240 align:start position:19%
gotten deeper.
So, the theories multiplied.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:06:01.280 align:start position:19%
Colliding neutron stars, black holes
shredding companion stars,

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:06.240 align:start position:19%
even cosmic strings, hypothetical cracks
in space-time itself.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:10.280 align:start position:19%
In 2017, Avi Loeb suggested the
repeating bursts could be leakage from

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:14.440 align:start position:19%
alien transmitters beaming power to
light sails.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:18.880 align:start position:19%
It made headlines worldwide.
But, every year the natural explanations

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:22.320 align:start position:19%
get stronger and the alien one gets
quieter.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:26.320 align:start position:19%
The universe is strange enough without
spaceships.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:31.480 align:start position:19%
And that strangeness turned out to be a
gift. Because as radio waves travel

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:36.919 align:start position:19%
across billions of light years, they get
smeared out by every electron they pass.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:41.080 align:start position:19%
And the smearing tells us exactly how
much invisible matter sits between us

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:45.919 align:start position:19%
and the burst.
Fast radio bursts have become cosmic

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:50.560 align:start position:19%
weighing scales, measuring the thin, hot
gas hiding between galaxies.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:55.000 align:start position:19%
They're helping solve one of cosmology's
biggest puzzles, the case of the

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:59.160 align:start position:19%
universe's missing matter.
It genuinely blows my mind that a

00:06:56.919 --> 00:07:04.040 align:start position:19%
mystery we could not explain became a
tool we cannot live without. These

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:09.280 align:start position:19%
millisecond screams are now mapping the
invisible skeleton of the cosmos.

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:13.320 align:start position:19%
The faint filaments of gas connecting
galaxies, the dark spaces between the

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:17.360 align:start position:19%
lights.
We spent years asking what fast radio

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:22.160 align:start position:19%
bursts are.
Now we are using them to ask what the

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:27.000 align:start position:19%
universe is made of.
Look closer at a burst and the physics

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:31.600 align:start position:19%
gets even wilder. Some flashes show
structure down to microseconds,

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:36.640 align:start position:19%
meaning the emitting region might be
only kilometers across.

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:41.280 align:start position:19%
The radio waves arrive twisted,
polarized by magnetic fields so extreme

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:47.000 align:start position:19%
they bend light itself.
Every burst is a laboratory for physics

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:50.840 align:start position:19%
we could never recreate on Earth.
And we are just getting started. New

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:54.720 align:start position:19%
observatories are coming online, arrays
with thousands of antennas that will

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:58.360 align:start position:19%
catch bursts by the thousands and
pinpoint their host galaxies

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:03.160 align:start position:19%
automatically. Within a few years, we
may go from hundreds of known bursts to

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:06.880 align:start position:19%
hundreds of thousands. The screaming sky
is about to get a lot louder, and

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:11.600 align:start position:19%
somewhere in that noise, the final
answer is waiting.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:16.520 align:start position:19%
Just think about this for a second.
Right now, as you watch this video,

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:23.840 align:start position:19%
thousands of these flashes are ripping
across the sky above you. Invisible,

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:27.720 align:start position:19%
silent, older than the Earth itself.
The universe is not the quiet, dark

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:33.000 align:start position:19%
place it seems.
It is loud, it is violent, it is

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:38.800 align:start position:19%
screaming in a language of radio light
that we only just learned to hear.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:42.640 align:start position:19%
If these millisecond screams from deep
space gave you chills, you're in the

00:08:41.039 --> 00:08:47.800 align:start position:19%
right place.
Cosmic Unknown explores a new cosmic

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:53.880 align:start position:19%
mystery every single day.
From black holes to alien worlds, hit

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:57.839 align:start position:19%
subscribe, turn on notifications, and
tell us in the comments

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:01.520 align:start position:19%
what do you think is screaming out
there?

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:04.839 align:start position:19%
We will see you in the next mystery
among the stars.

